---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CommercialContinuousScwAseExtractionModeler from '../../components/CommercialContinuousScwAseExtractionModeler.astro';

const pageTitle = "Commercial Continuous Subcritical Water Dynamic Accelerated Solvent Extraction (SCW-ASE) Modeler | IsItKitchenSafe";
const pageDescription = "Simulate commercial continuous subcritical water dynamic accelerated solvent extraction (SCW-ASE, 120–220°C, 30–80 bar) with in-line ceramic cross-flow microfiltration for zero-solvent recovery of polyphenols, flavonoids, and antioxidants under ASME Section VIII and 3-A Sanitary Standards.";

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Tools', href: '/tools/' },
  { label: 'Commercial Continuous SCW-ASE Extraction Modeler' }
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "How does Subcritical Water Dynamic Accelerated Solvent Extraction (SCW-ASE) dissolve semi-polar and non-polar polyphenols without organic solvents?",
    answer: "At ambient temperature (25°C), water is an intensely polar solvent with a high dielectric constant (ε ≈ 78.5) that cannot dissolve lipophilic or semi-polar secondary plant metabolites like quercetin, resveratrol, and proanthocyanidins. In subcritical water extraction (also known as Pressurized Hot Water Extraction or PHWE), liquid water is heated between 120°C and 220°C under moderate pressure (30–80 bar / 3–8 MPa) to keep it in the sub-boiling liquid phase. Under these hydrothermal conditions, thermal expansion weakens hydrogen bonding, causing water's dielectric constant to drop sharply to between ε ≈ 30 and 35. This matches the dielectric permittivity of organic solvents like ethanol (ε ≈ 24.3) and methanol (ε ≈ 32.7), allowing hot pressurized water to dissolve and extract semi-polar flavonoids, catechins, and phenolic acids cleanly, leaving 0.00 ppm volatile organic solvent (VOC) residues."
  },
  {
    question: "How does in-line high-temperature ceramic cross-flow microfiltration clarify the extract without cooling or blinding?",
    answer: "Conventional botanical extraction skids require batch cooling, multi-stage centrifugation, and filter aids (diatomaceous earth) to separate spent plant fibers from liquid extract—adding labor, capital cost, and thermal exposure. The continuous SCW skid integrates an in-line multichannel ceramic cross-flow membrane (0.14 µm α-Al2O3 / zirconia core) operating directly at high temperature (up to 240°C) and operating pressure (up to 80 bar). The tangential shear velocity of the fluid across the ceramic lumen prevents cake compaction and concentration polarization. Suspended cellular debris, colloidal pectins, and insoluble cellulosic fibers are retained (>99.9%), producing a brilliant, optical-grade permeate directly upstream of the backpressure regulator without fiber shedding or membrane fouling."
  },
  {
    question: "Why is instantaneous counter-current cryogenic quench cooling critical for preserving bioactive potency?",
    answer: "Although high temperatures (120–220°C) accelerate mass transfer coefficients and enhance phytochemical solubility, thermolabile bioactives—such as cyanidin-3-glucosides in berry pomace and EGCG in tea leaves—are susceptible to rapid thermal degradation and oxidative epimerization following Arrhenius first-order kinetics. At 150°C, the thermal degradation half-life (t₁/₂) of delicate anthocyanins drops to under 18 minutes. Continuous SCW skids prevent thermal degradation by maintaining dynamic contact time to just 2 to 5 minutes, immediately followed by an in-line sanitary chilled glycol quench heat exchanger. The pressurized product stream is chilled from ~160°C down to below 25°C in under 2.2 seconds (or <1.2 s with microchannel quenchers), halting thermal degradation and preserving >96% of active antioxidant capacity (ORAC) and native coloration."
  },
  {
    question: "What is auto-catalytic hydrothermal deglycosylation and how does it enhance flavonoid bioavailability?",
    answer: "Many valuable dietary flavonoids naturally exist in plant tissues as bulky glycosides (e.g., rutin, quercetin-3-rutinoside, hesperidin) that exhibit low intestinal permeability and bioavailability compared to their free aglycone forms. As subcritical water is heated to 160–190°C, the ionization product of water (Kw) increases by over three orders of magnitude, causing the pKw to drop from 14.00 down to approximately 11.20. This drastic rise in self-generated hydronium (H3O+) and hydroxide (OH-) ions provides an in-situ auto-catalytic acidic/basic hydrothermal environment. In red onion skin or citrus peel slurries, these ions selectively cleave the β-glycosidic linkages between the sugar moiety and the phenolic core, transforming glycosides into pure, highly bioavailable aglycones (such as quercetin aglycone) in under 4 minutes without requiring synthetic mineral acids, enzymes, or hazardous chemical catalysts."
  },
  {
    question: "How does the continuous SCW skid comply with ASME Section VIII Div 1, 3-A Sanitary Standards 04-05 & 44-03, and EHEDG Class I aseptic guidelines?",
    answer: "Continuous subcritical water processing operates at the intersection of high-pressure fluid engineering and sanitary food manufacturing: (1) Under the ASME Boiler & Pressure Vessel Code (Section VIII Div 1), all extraction vessels, tubular preheaters, and ceramic membrane housings are forged from electropolished AISI 316L (UNS S31603) or solid Hastelloy C-276, rated for a Maximum Allowable Working Pressure (MAWP) of 85–120 bar with mandatory ASME burst rupture disks; (2) In compliance with 3-A Standard 04-05, the high-pressure triplex feed pump utilizes food-grade solid ceramic plungers and USP Class VI perfluoroelastomer packings; (3) Under 3-A Standard 44-03, all product-contact piping has an internal electropolished finish of Ra ≤ 0.38 µm (15 µ-in) with positive drainability; and (4) The closed sanitary loop is validated for automated Clean-In-Place (CIP) and Steam-In-Place (SIP, 130°C for 30 minutes) under FDA 21 CFR § 117 cGMP."
  }
];

const structuredData = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "WebApplication",
      "@id": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/commercial-continuous-scw-ase-extraction-modeler/#webapp",
      "url": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/commercial-continuous-scw-ase-extraction-modeler/",
      "name": "Commercial Continuous Subcritical Water Dynamic Accelerated Solvent Extraction (SCW-ASE) Modeler",
      "applicationCategory": "EngineeringApplication",
      "operatingSystem": "All",
      "browserRequirements": "Requires JavaScript. Requires HTML5 Canvas/CSS3.",
      "offers": {
        "@type": "Offer",
        "price": "0",
        "priceCurrency": "USD"
      },
      "description": pageDescription
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "itemListElement": [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 1,
          "name": "Home",
          "item": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 2,
          "name": "Tools",
          "item": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/"
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          "position": 3,
          "name": "Commercial Continuous SCW-ASE Extraction Modeler",
          "item": "https://isitkitchensafe.com/tools/commercial-continuous-scw-ase-extraction-modeler/"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      "mainEntity": faqs.map(faq => ({
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": faq.question,
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": faq.answer
        }
      }))
    }
  ]
};
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription}>
  <div class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-6">
    <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} />

    <CommercialContinuousScwAseExtractionModeler lang="en" />

    <!-- FAQ Accordion / Section -->
    <section class="mt-16 border-t border-hairline pt-12 max-w-4xl mx-auto">
      <div class="text-center mb-10">
        <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-tight">
          Frequently Asked Questions: Subcritical Water Dynamic Botanical Extraction
        </h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-muted mt-2">
          Engineering guidelines, hydrothermal physics, and sanitary standards for commercial continuous subcritical water extraction.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        {faqs.map((faq, index) => (
          <article class="bg-surface-soft border border-hairline rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-xs">
            <h3 class="text-base sm:text-lg font-bold text-ink mb-2">
              {index + 1}. {faq.question}
            </h3>
            <p class="text-sm text-body leading-relaxed">
              {faq.answer}
            </p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(structuredData)} />
</Layout>
